    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d470">

                <pb facs="0644" n="520" sameAs="frus:frus1923v02/pg520"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.50a/7: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in the Netherlands</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sussdorff</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-03-21T18:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 21,
                                1923—6 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>16. The Department desires you to inform the Minister for Foreign Affairs of
                    Luxemburg that it is the intention of the Government of the United States to
                    accredit as its representative near the Grand Ducal Government the American
                    diplomatic representative at Brussels <pb facs="0645" n="521" xml:id="pg521"/>
                    instead of the representative at The Hague, as has heretofore been the practice;
                    and also that to this end you are directed to request the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">agrément</hi> of Mr. Fletcher, whom the President
                    desires to appoint as Minister to Luxemburg. If the Government of Luxemburg
                    request to be informed through what channels it may communicate with the
                    Department during the interval before Mr. Fletcher presents his letter of
                        credence,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d470-f1"/> you may reply that it may continue
                    to send its communications through the Legation at The Hague and addressed to
                    yourself as Chargé d’Affaires ad interim. This duty will not entitle you to
                    additional compensation and you will not visit Luxemburg without the express
                    authorization of the Department.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d470-f1" n="1" xml:id="d470-f1-content">
                <p>Mr. Fletcher presented his letter of credence on Oct. 29, 1923.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
